{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.276,
            0.085,
            0.901,
            0.152
          ],
          "content": "**You must have observed that magnesium ribbon burns with a dazzling white flame and changes into a white powder. This powder is magnesium oxide. It is formed due to the reaction between magnesium and oxygen present in the air.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.104,
            0.165,
            0.258,
            0.186
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Activity 1.2"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.1,
            0.2,
            0.261,
            0.244
          ],
          "content": "**Take lead nitrate solution in a test tube.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.101,
            0.245,
            0.261,
            0.288
          ],
          "content": "**Add potassium iodide solution to this.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.1,
            0.289,
            0.261,
            0.317
          ],
          "content": "**What do you observe?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.076,
            0.327,
            0.401,
            0.555
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.077,
                0.578,
                0.254,
                0.648
              ],
              "content": "Figure 1.2 Formation of hydrogen gas by the action of dilute sulphuric acid on zinc"
            }
          ],
          "image_source": "images/page_1_image_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.366,
            0.167,
            0.521,
            0.19
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Activity 1.3"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.348,
            0.201,
            0.858,
            0.216
          ],
          "content": "n **Take a few zinc granules in a conical flask or a test tube.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.349,
            0.216,
            0.875,
            0.246
          ],
          "content": "**Add dilute hydrochloric acid or sulphuric acid to this (Fig. 1.2).**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.372,
            0.244,
            0.691,
            0.26
          ],
          "content": "**CAUTION: Handle the acid with care.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.349,
            0.26,
            0.874,
            0.289
          ],
          "content": "**Do you observe anything happening around the zinc granules?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.349,
            0.289,
            0.875,
            0.319
          ],
          "content": "**Touch the conical flask or test tube. Is there any change in its temperature?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.414,
            0.329,
            0.9,
            0.378
          ],
          "content": "**From the above three activities, we can say that any of the following observations helps us to determine whether a chemical reaction has taken place –**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.446,
            0.386,
            0.615,
            0.401
          ],
          "content": "n **change in state**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.447,
            0.402,
            0.629,
            0.417
          ],
          "content": "n **change in colour**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.448,
            0.418,
            0.635,
            0.433
          ],
          "content": "n **evolution of a gas**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.447,
            0.434,
            0.684,
            0.45
          ],
          "content": "n **change in temperature.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.413,
            0.457,
            0.9,
            0.539
          ],
          "content": "**As we observe the changes around us, we can see that there is a large variety of chemical reactions taking place around us. We will study about the various types of chemical reactions and their symbolic representation in this Chapter.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.279,
            0.567,
            0.599,
            0.588
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "1.1 CHEMICAL EQUA TIONS"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.276,
            0.596,
            0.901,
            0.677
          ],
          "content": "**Activity 1.1 can be described as – when a magnesium ribbon is burnt in oxygen, it gets converted to magnesium oxide. This description of a chemical reaction in a sentence form is quite long. It can be written in a shorter form. The simplest way to do this is to write it in the form of a word-equation.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.278,
            0.677,
            0.747,
            0.694
          ],
          "content": "**The word-equation for the above reaction would be –**"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.33,
            0.7,
            0.897,
            0.733
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{array} { c c c } { { \\mathrm { M a g n e s i u m } + \\mathrm { O x y g e n } } } & { { \\rightarrow } } & { { \\mathrm { M a g n e s i u m ~ o x i d e } } } \\\\ { { \\mathrm { ( R e a c t a n t s ) } } } & { { } } & { { \\mathrm { ( P r o d u c t ) } } } \\end{array}\\tag{1.1}",
          "image_source": "images/page_1_equation_22.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.277,
            0.74,
            0.899,
            0.789
          ],
          "content": "**The substances that undergo chemical change in the reaction (1.1), magnesium and oxygen, are the reactants. The new substance is magnesium oxide, formed during the reaction, as a product.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.276,
            0.789,
            0.901,
            0.886
          ],
          "content": "**A word-equation shows change of reactants to products through an arrow placed between them. The reactants are written on the left-hand side (LHS) with a plus sign (+) between them. Similarly, products are written on the right-hand side (RHS) with a plus sign (+) between them. The arrowhead points towards the products, and shows the direction of the reaction.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
